# Universe content · moons
# researched: Wikipedia "Regular moon", "Natural satellite"; Oxford "Moons: A Very Short Introduction";
#   university astronomy lectures (Oregon, Ohio State) on ring and moon systems
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r05-universe.md · wiki/systems/r06-planet.md

# ── By how they formed ──

@moon regular
name: Regular moon
real: yes
says: formed in the disk around its planet, like a mini solar system; close, round, flat orbits

@moon irregular
name: Irregular moon
real: yes
says: a captured asteroid or icy body on a far, tilted, stretched or backward orbit
example: Triton, Phobos, Deimos

@moon transitional
name: Transitional moon
real: yes
says: between regular and irregular
example: Iapetus

@moon impact-moon
name: Impact moon
real: yes
says: formed from the debris of a giant collision with its planet
example: Earth's Moon

@moon moonlet
name: Moonlet
real: yes
says: a tiny moon, often inside a ring

# ── By surface and inside ──

@moon rocky-airless
name: Rocky airless moon
real: yes
says: cratered rock, no air

@moon volcanic
name: Volcanic moon
real: yes
says: tides squeeze it until it erupts constantly, maybe over a magma ocean
example: Io

@moon ice-shell-ocean
name: Ice-shell ocean moon
real: yes
says: a crust of ice over a global ocean of liquid water, kept warm by tides; a possible home for life
example: Europa, Enceladus

@moon cryovolcanic
name: Cryovolcanic moon
real: yes
says: shoots geysers of water vapor and ice through cracks in its shell
example: Enceladus

@moon thick-air
name: Thick-air moon
real: yes
says: a moon with heavy air, weather, and lakes and rain of liquid hydrocarbons
example: Titan

@moon icy-dead
name: Frozen dead moon
real: yes
says: ice and rock, no activity, ancient cratered surface

@moon habitable
name: Habitable moon
real: hypothetical
says: an Earth-like moon orbiting a giant planet in its star's habitable zone

# ── By orbit ──

@moon tidally-locked
name: Tidally locked moon
real: yes
says: always shows the same face to its planet; most big moons are locked

@moon shepherd
name: Shepherd moon
real: yes
says: a small moon whose pull herds a planet's ring into a sharp band

@moon trojan
name: Trojan moon
real: yes
says: a small moon sharing a bigger moon's orbit, 60° ahead or behind it
example: the trojan moons of Tethys and Dione at Saturn

@moon resonant
name: Resonant moons
real: yes
says: moons whose orbits keep a steady beat with each other, squeezing each other with tides
example: Io, Europa and Ganymede (1:2:4)

@moon retrograde
name: Retrograde moon
real: yes
says: orbits backward, against its planet's spin; usually captured
example: Triton

@moon sub-moon
name: Moon of a moon
real: hypothetical
says: a tiny moon circling a moon; none confirmed

# ── Generator rules ──

@rule moon-seed
real: rule
says: every moon is generated live from the world seed; nothing is pre-built

@rule moon-count
real: rule
says: giants collect many moons, both regular and captured; rocky planets have few or none

@rule moon-tides
real: rule
says: tides from the planet and from neighboring moons heat moons from inside, powering volcanoes and keeping oceans liquid

@rule moon-hill-sphere
real: rule
says: a moon only stays inside its planet's sphere of pull (content/solar-systems.dnat)

@rule moon-capture
real: rule
says: passing asteroids and comets can be captured as irregular moons

@rule moon-life
real: rule
says: ocean moons and thick-air moons can hold life; a moon's local conditions use the same 10-condition world matrix as planets
